Mniku (Chapel Island), Potlotek First Nation, Cape Breton, Nova Scotia

&nbsp;

Mniku is approximately 200 m offshore at the north end of Sitmuk Awti (Chapel Island Road), off of Highway 4, in Potlotek. The Project site is located along the eastern shoreline of the southern portion of Mniku, and is accessible by way of watercraft launched at a small wharf located at the north end of Sitmuk Awti.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Mniku is a National Historic Site of cultural, spiritual, and archaeological importance to the Mi&#39;kmaq. Portions of the Mniku shoreline are lowlying and vulnerable to coastal erosion and flooding. Shoreline retreat has resulted in the exposure of sacred burial grounds and culturally significant artifacts, highlighting the sensitivity of the site and the need for erosion mitigation measures that protect both physical landforms and irreplaceable cultural heritage. Coastal hazards at Mniku have intensified over time due to increasing storm activity, elevated water levels, and climate change impacts, with notable erosion damage occurring during major storm events such as Hurricane Fiona in September 2022. These processes continue to pose a risk to culturally sensitive shoreline areas, particularly along shores actively used for community and ceremonial activities.

&nbsp;

The proposed design is considered a hybrid nature-based infrastructure solution which pairs a rock revetment along the shoreline on Mniku with planted vegetation features on the crest and landward side of the revetment. The entire revetment structure will be constructed on the landward side of the delineated Eelgrass habitats along the shoreline of Mniku to avoid directly impacting these habitats, and will be constructed above the higher-high water large tide elevation for this site. There are no planned site preparation activities along the shoreline due to the presence of graves and archaeological resources; the materials used to construct the revetment will be placed directly overtop of the existing shoreline materials, and there are no requirements to relocate existing structures or vegetation along the existing shoreline. The total footprint area of the structure is 650 m2.

&nbsp;

Eskasoni First Nation, Cape Breton, Nova Scotia

&nbsp;

The physical activity at this location is to be carried out on federal lands in relation to a physical work and requires a&nbsp;Canadian Navigable Waters Act (CNWA) approval. Therefore, Transport Canada is a federal authority for the Eskasoni location only.

&nbsp;

Portions of the Eskasoni shoreline along Shore Road are relatively low-lying and are vulnerable to coastal erosion and flooding. Erosion of the shoreline has led to shoreline retreat and over time this process has the potential to pose a risk to the portion of the shore in the project footprint area which is used recreationally (e.g., swimming, fishing) by the community.

&nbsp;

The proposed design is considered a hybrid nature-based infrastructure solution that pairs three low-crested rock sills with landward sediment infill and planted vegetation features on the landward side of the revetment. The sills dissipate wave energy generated within the Bras d&#39;Or Lake before it reaches the shoreline which reduces the erosive forces acting on the shoreline and backshore areas. Small gaps between each sill allow for water circulation and sediment exchange while maintaining effective wave attenuation. This proposed configuration of sills and sediment infill is well suited to the mild to moderate, fetch-limited wave conditions present at the site.

&nbsp;

The proposed Project design avoids overlap or interaction with the delineated wetland or Eelgrass habitats during or following construction. Overall, the project footprint area for the three sills and coarse sand infill is 980 m2.

The Department of National Defence (DND) is proposing a project. DND is undertaking radar installations in the form of to calibrate and validate over-the-horizon radar technology for use in Canada. The DND Property covers an area of approximately 20 ha. It consists primarily of an open mowed grass field with seven communication antennae present. There is a paved road leading to a fenced compound where a two-story receiver building is located, just west of the center of the property. There is a small concrete block outbuilding, reportedly used as a shed, adjacent to the south of the receiver building. There are two above ground fuel storage tanks (AST) located on the Project Site. There is a stand of trees that is not being disturbed. The Project Site is accessible from Loyalist Parkway. Development at the site will focus on installation of receive monopoles, consistent with this installation. Land preparation is minimal as the site is a developed and active antenna receive site. Preparation will include minimal flattening removal of previous concrete antenna anchors and a few shrubs. The construction footprint of the Project is approximately 3.25 ha.
While specific infrastructure needs are being finalized, the Arctic Over the Horizon Radar (A-OTHR) system will require supporting infrastructure at each site (these are existing onsite):


	Receive antenna monopoles;
	Temporary Sea container to house equipment;
	Physical security features;
	Utilities (power &amp; communications).


The anticipated project start date for site preparation is: August 2026.
The anticipated in-service date (operations) is: tentatively December 2026.

The Department of National Defence (DND) is proposing a project. DND is undertaking radar installations in the form of to calibrate and validate over-the-horizon radar technology for use in Canada. The DND Property covers an area of approximately 65 ha immediately north of the shore of Lake Ontario. The natural environment at the Property consists of cultural meadow, landscape lawn, alvar type communities, wetlands and the beach along the shoreline. The Project Site is accessible from Point Petre Road. Development at the site will focus on installation of 2 transmitting antennas consistent with the development of the property. Land preparation is minimal as it has been cleared and prepared decades ago and actively maintained. The construction footprint of the Project is approximately 11.3 ha.
While specific infrastructure needs are being finalized, the Arctic Over the Horizon Radar (A-OTHR) system will require supporting infrastructure at each site (these are existing onsite):


	Temporary building (sea container) to house equipment;
	Physical security features;
	Access road; and;
	Utilities (power &amp; communications).


The anticipated project start date for site preparation is: August 2026.
The anticipated in-service date (operations) is: December 2026.

ATCO Gas and Pipelines Ltd. is planning to relocate approximately 500m of 60.3mm diameter polyethylene gas main at the request of Tsuut&#39;ina Nation to accommodate a proposed Medical Centre currently in conflict with the existing gas main. The gas main will be installed at a depth of 0.8m - 1.1m below ground surface and will be installed primarily using horizontal diagonal drilling (HDD) and plow-in methods.

&nbsp;

Construction will include removal of topsoil, installation of gas main, back filling with original material, reclamation, and commissioning by flaring a small volume of sweet natural gas.

&nbsp;

One wetland (seasonal graminoid marsh) is located within 30m of the site project. The project will include the pre-construction wildlife sweeps.

Fisheries and Oceans Canada (DFO) intends to complete repairs and upgrades to the existing access road serving the Nashwaak Fish Counting Station located at 1280 Route 148, New Brunswick.

The existing unpaved access road traverses predominantly wetland terrain and is subject to seasonal flooding, surface water flow, and significant washouts. Several sections of the road are approaching impassable conditions for passenger vehicles due to erosion and inadequate drainage .

The proposed project includes:


	Regrading of the existing roadway to improve profile and drainage; 
	Installation of a geogrid-reinforced roadway structure 
	Construction of drainage ditches to lower the water table and improve road performance; 
	Installation of multiple culverts (HDPE) to accommodate seasonal and stormwater flows; 
	Placement of embankment fill; 
	Installation of geotextile beneath embankments where required; 
	Installation of two swing gates to restrict public access to the fish counting station; 
	Associated works including clearing, grubbing, erosion control, and site stabilization. 


The project is designed to improve safe access while minimizing impacts to surrounding wetlands. However, some permanent footprint within wetland areas is anticipated, and compensation measures may be required .

Construction activities will involve earthworks, excavation, placement of fill materials, and water management measures. Temporary groundwater and surface water infiltration is expected during construction and will be managed through appropriate drainage controls.

The Department of National Defence (DND) is proposing a project. The Temporary Modular Workspaces (TMW) scope of work includes construction of new one-story, partially prefabricated trailer buildings. The objective is to provide temporary accommodations for staff currently using Buildings MA26 and MB38, during construction activities in these buildings. The planned configurations of the TMW buildings are detailed as follows:


	One (1) building with a footprint of 910 m2;
	One (1) building with a footprint of 856 m2;
	One (1) building with a footprint of 776 m2;
	Two (2) buildings each with a footprint of 307 m2.


Each TMW building will connect to utility infrastructure services existing on (or adjacent) the site. Installation of new utilities includes electricity, communications, water and sanitary/storm services. While the majority of utility infrastructure will be installed below ground, new poles will be installed for connection to electricity. A new access route (road) will be constructed from Craftsman Boulevard at the south aspect of the site, to an existing parking lot northeast of the site. Temporary walkways for employee access and snow clearing will also be created. Due to the shallow bedrock at the site location, the buildings are anticipated to be constructed upon a foundation of strip footings and rock anchors. Construction activities for the project are expected to commence Fall 2026, for completion by Fall 2027.

The Lorneville Small Craft Harbour (SCH) is situated on a natural promontory extending northward into Lorneville Harbour.&nbsp; The harbour is further protected by a 165 m-long rubblemound breakwater (SCH Facility Number (#) 301) that extends out from the headland.&nbsp; The existing public wharf (#401) is a tee-shaped timber pile structure located on the lee side of the breakwater.&nbsp; The structure consists of square-post trestle bents along the approach stem and a timber pile bent system at the tee section.&nbsp; The wharf is more than 60 years old and has reached the end of its service life.

&nbsp;

The proposed works will involve the complete demolition and removal of the wharf structure.&nbsp; Demolition activities will likely be undertaken using land-based construction methods, either from a temporary earthen platform constructed along the interior side of the existing breakwater (#301) or by installation of a temporary trestle bridge.&nbsp; These approaches will facilitate the use of land-based equipment, including long-reach excavators, cranes, and haul trucks operating in dry conditions.&nbsp; Alternatively, the methodology could also involve a barge operation with equipment such as a crane and/or excavator operating from the floating platform to dismantle the wharf and relay material to the upland.

&nbsp;

The existing structure will likely be dismantled in sections or segments and temporarily placed along the approach road for further processing and separation.&nbsp; Materials suitable for recycling or reuse will be salvaged where practical, while unsuitable materials will be managed as waste, loaded onto trucks, and transported to provincially-approved disposal facilities.&nbsp; Demolition activities will also involve the removal of ballast boxes and stone and may also require incidental excavation of underwater sediments.&nbsp; Following completion of demolition activities, all temporary work areas and disturbed ground surfaces will be stabilized as required, with ballast and earthen materials/rock from temporary access reworked into the existing breakwater.&nbsp; All construction equipment and construction waste materials will be removed from the site.

&nbsp;

Demolition activities are anticipated to commence in November 2026 and be completed by the end of March 2027.&nbsp; Project commencement is, however, subject to DFO-SCH operational priorities and funding, as well as the completion of regulatory approval processes, therefore work may be delayed.&nbsp; The scope of the environmental effects determination (subject to S.82 of the Impact Assessment Act) will, therefore, be based on the works being completed over a period of three (3) months commencing within a period of 5 years following the determination of significance of environmental effects.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

The Lorneville SCH is a fishing harbour that was originally developed to serve the fishing industry in the area.&nbsp; The site has been used as a fishing harbour for several decades. Departmental records indicate that a breakwater has been in place since at least the late 1800s.&nbsp; Its well-protected location provides easy access to the fishing grounds of the Bay of Fundy.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Given that the existing wharf is well beyond its service life and load restricted, the status quo of leaving the deteriorated structures available and in service represents a contingent liability to the Crown.&nbsp; Repairs are not practical and complete reconstruction would be necessary to provide the same level of service.

&nbsp;

The four (4) homeport vessels at the Lorneville SCH have relocated to the Five Fathom Harbour SCH (approximately 8.5 kms to the northwest) due to the harbour&#39;s deteriorated condition.&nbsp; Following review of the costs associated with reconstruction of the wharf at Lorneville, DFO-SCH decided that permanent accommodation of these users at Five Fathom Harbour SCH through the installation of an additional floating dock was the most practicable and cost-effective long-term option.

Crown Mountain Resources Ltd. is proposing to construct and operate an open-pit metallurgical coal mine in the Elk Valley, approximately 12&nbsp;kilometres from the District of Sparwood in southeastern British Columbia. As proposed, the production capacity of the project would be approximately 2.0 million tonnes per year, over a mine-life of about 15 years.

&nbsp;

Please note that the proponent for this project was previously NWP Coal Canada Ltd.

The proposed project consists of constructing a paved Ground Support Equipment (GSE) parking and staging area on previously developed airport lands adjacent to the terminal building and existing apron. The work includes pavement construction, grading, stormwater management infrastructure, subdrain installation, utility servicing for future electric GSE charging infrastructure, lighting modifications, and associated site works. The project will support airport ground operations, improve equipment storage and circulation. The total expansion area is approximately 2,200 m2.
&nbsp;
During construction, stormwater runoff and sediment control measures will be provided by the site contractor, as required, and reviewed by the Airport Authority on a regular basis. All municipal, provincial and federal codes, standards, and best management practices will be followed. The expansion is within previously developed areas (i.e., existing GSE area or landscaped area) with no woodland or known sensitive ecological habitat being impacted.

ATCO Gas and Pipelines Ltd. is planning to install 100 meters of 60 millimeter diameter polyethylene gas main and 65 meters of 42 millimeter gas service line at Blood Tribe. The new distribution gas main will accommodate a proposed new administration building in Standoff adjacent to the Kainai Wellness Center. The proposed gas line will be installed at a depth of 0.8 to 1.2 meters and will be tied into the existing gas main. The proposed methods of installation will include trenching, as well as horizontal directional drilling and use of hydrovac when required.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Construction will include removal of topsoil, installation of gas main, back filling, re-grading, reclamation, and flaring of the gas to ensure the gas line has 100 percent gas in it.

&nbsp;

No vegetation clearing is proposed and the project is not located within 30 meters of any waterbodies or wetlands. Wildlife species at risk are not anticipated to be present in the project construction area. A wildlife and nest sweep will be completed prior to construction.

ATCO Gas and Pipelines Ltd. is planning to install 830 meters of 60 millimeter diameter polyethylene gas main and six short 42 millimeter gas services at Blood Tribe on previously cleared lands. The new distribution gas main will accommodate a proposed commercial subdivision on the eastern edge of Standoff. The proposed gas line will be installed at a depth of 0.8 to 1.2 meters and will be tied into the existing gas main. The proposed methods of installation will include trenching, as well as horizontal directional drilling and use of hydrovac when required.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Construction will include removal of topsoil, installation of gas main, back filling, re-grading, reclamation, and flaring of the gas to ensure the gas line has 100 percent gas in it.

&nbsp;

No vegetation clearing is proposed and the project is not located near any waterbodies or wetlands. Wildlife species at risk are not anticipated to be present in the project construction area. A wildlife and nest sweep will be completed prior to construction.

The Proposed Project: Laydown and Logistics areas&nbsp;

As part of the Greater Toronto Airports Authority&#39;s (GTAA&#39;s) Toronto Pearson Airport Longterm Investment in Facilities &amp; Terminals (LIFT) Program,&nbsp;logistics&nbsp;and laydown areas will be constructed on Airport Lands, to&nbsp;facilitate&nbsp;construction needs.&nbsp;These sites will be developed on&nbsp;unmaintained&nbsp;vegetated&nbsp;areas&nbsp;located&nbsp;both airside and groundside&nbsp;on&nbsp;airport&nbsp;property.&nbsp;The sites will consist of&nbsp;a combination of any of the following:&nbsp;temporary construction trailers, worker parking lot, material laydown, equipment storage, and associated infrastructure to support Airport construction projects.&nbsp;

The sites will undergo a variety of improvements, depending on&nbsp;the current conditions and&nbsp;the requirements for construction.&nbsp;Activities may include, as&nbsp;required,&nbsp;vegetation removal,&nbsp;minor excavation, clearing and grubbing, utility removals /relocations including the addition of temporary pole-mounted lighting with overhead power, re-grading , placement of aggregate and compact areas where required, construction trailers, security booths, transportation services for workers, paving and line painting of parking and trailer areas, stormwater management, and other associated infrastructure. No new permanent buildings are proposed. Trailers will be prefabricated units delivered to the site.&nbsp;&nbsp;

Several sustainability best practices will be incorporated into the projects design and implementation for the wider Toronto Pearson Airport Longterm Investment in Facilities &amp; Terminals (LIFT) Program.
